/**
 * # Webhooks

Webhooks allow you to build or set up integrations, such as OAuth Apps, which subscribe to certain events on api.clubmed.com.
When one of those events is triggered, we'll send a HTTP POST payload to the webhook's configured URL.

Webhooks can be used to update an external information like the `room_status`. You're only limited by your imagination.

## Configuration

A webhook can be configured on one or multiple events by using the admin routes `POST /v0/api_webhooks` and needs some information like the `Payload URL`, `Content-Type`, etc...

### Payload URL

The payload URL is the URL of the server that will receive the webhook POST requests.

### Content type

Webhooks can be delivered using different content types:

- The `application/json` content type will deliver the JSON payload directly as the body of the POST request.
- The `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` content type will send the JSON payload as a form parameter called payload.

Choose the one that best fits your needs.

### Secret

Setting a webhook secret allows you to ensure that `POST` requests sent to the payload URL are from Clubmed.
When you set a secret, you'll receive the `X-Clubmed-Signature` header in the webhook `POST` request.

Here is an example in Node.js to secure your payload:

```js
import { createHmac } from 'crypto';
import express from 'express';

const app = express();

function getSignature(body, secret) {
  const hmac = createHmac('sha256', secret)
   .update(body)
   .digest('hex');

   return `sha256=${hmac}`;
}

function rawBodyMiddleware(request, response, next) {
  request.rawBody = "";
  request.on("data", (chunk: string) => {
  request.rawBody += chunk;
});

app.post('/api/callback', rawBodyMiddleware, (request, response) => {
   const signature = request.get('X-Clubmed-Signature')

   if (getSignature(request.rawBody, YOUR_SECRET_KEY) !== signature) {
     throw new Error('Bad signature');
   }

   const body = JSON.parse(payload);
   console.log(body);
});
```

### Events

You are able to choose between all of the followings events or use `*` to listen all Clubmed API events.

<div class="overflow-auto" style="max-height: 400px">

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `data_updated`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">Trigger an event when the date change</small>

#### Payload

By listening to this event, you'll receive the following payload:

```json
{
  "event": "data_updated",
  "locale": "fr-FR",
  "paths": [
    {
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/v0/products/{product_id}/cruise_detail"
    }
  ]
}
```

</div>

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `intervention_status`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">Trigger an event when an intervention is given a status update</small>

MaintOtel with its application Etis triggers an event to the Clubmed API when there is a status' update for a given
booking intervention. Then the API emits the `intervention_status` event so the consumers can react to it.

#### Payload

By listening to this event, you'll receive the given payload:

```json
{
    "product_id": "CANC",
    "booking_id": "234567",
    "room_id" : "B155",
    "status" : "PENDING"
}
```

</div>

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `notification_end`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">Trigger an event when a notification is ended</small>

#### Payload

By listening to this event, you'll receive the following payload:

```json
{
  "id": ""
  "request_id": ""
  "type": "BARS",
  "event": "",
  "locales": ["fr-FR"],
  "product_id": "optional"
}
```

type | related API Endpoint
---|---
`GO` | `/v0/products/{products_id}/go_staff`
`PRODUCTS` | `/v0/products/{products_id}`
`BARS` | `/v1/products/{products_id}/bars`
`RESTAURANTS` | `/v1/products/{products_id}/restaurants`
`CHILDCARE` | `/v1/products/{products_id}/childcare`
`INTERVENTION_CATEGORIES` | `/v0/products/{product_id}/intervention_categories`
`PRACTICAL_INFO` | `/v1/products/{product_id}/practical_information`
`RESORT_PLANNING` | `/v0/products/{product_id}/resort_planning`

</div>

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `ping`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">A simple webhook event to test your Webhook integration with the Clubmed API</small>

When you create a new webhook, we'll send you a simple ping event to let you know you've set up the webhook correctly.
Call the `POST /v0/api_webhooks/ping` route to trigger the `ping` event with your custom payload.

#### Payload

```json
{
  // your payload
}
```

</div>

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `planning`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">Trigger an event when the planning information are changed by an eGO</small>

eGo can change the activity, dresscode and his related timeslots every time. By listening this event, you'll be notified when a change occurs on the activity or dresscode planning.

### Deprecated

This event is deprecated! Use the `notification_end` event instead.

#### Payload

By listening to this event, you'll receive the following payload:

```json
{
  "locale": "fr-FR",
  "event": "CMS_DRUPAL",  // arbitraire
  "product_id": "MPAC"
}
```

</div>

<div class="bg-white rounded-small px-3 pt-1 pb-5 mb-5">

### Event `room_status`

<small class="text-sm text-gray-lighter -mt-3 block" style="color: #777">Trigger an event when the room is ready for a booking</small>

The PMS in resort triggers an event to the Clubmed API when a room is available for the customer then the Clubmed API emit
the `room_status` event.

<div class="text-center">
  <img src="/doc/img/schemes/room-status-webhook-workflow.png" alt="Room ready workflow" style="max-width: 80%" />
</div>

#### Payload

By listening to this event, you'll receive the following payload:

```json
{
  "booking_id": "24356778",
  "customer_id": "1234567",
  "room_id": "B240",
  "status": "READY"
}
```

</div>


</div>

## Ping and Logs

When you create a new webhook, we'll send you a simple ping event to let you know you've set up the webhook correctly.
Call the `POST /v0/api_webhooks/ping` route to trigger the `ping` event with your custom payload then call `GET /v0/api_webhooks/logs` to see
all logs events registered by the Clubmed API.

## Exposing localhost to the internet

To test your webhook integration with your local application, you can use `ngrok` to expose your local application on internet.
We'll use ngrok to do this. ngrok is available, free of charge, for all major operating systems. For more information, see the [ngrok download page](https://ngrok.com/download).

After installing ngrok, you can expose your localhost by running `ngrok http 4567` on the command line. `4567` is the port number on which our server will listen for messages.
You should see a line that looks something like this:

```
$ Forwarding http://7e9ea9dc.ngrok.io -> 127.0.0.1:4567
```

Use `http://7e9ea9dc.ngrok.io` url as Payload Url for your webhook configuration:

```
PATCH /v0/api_webhooks/your_uuid_configuration

{
  "url": "http://7e9ea9dc.ngrok.io"
}
```

Then call the route `POST /v0/api_webhooks/ping`.


 * @summary Returns all available events
 */
export const getGetV0ApiWebhooksEventsUrl = () => {
  return `/v0/api_webhooks/events`;
};

export const getV0ApiWebhooksEvents = async (
  options?: RequestInit
): Promise<ApiWebhookEventsModel> => {
  return fetcher<Promise<ApiWebhookEventsModel>>(
    getGetV0ApiWebhooksEventsUrl(),
    {
      ...options,
      method: "GET",
    }
  );
};

